This study explores combined distributions involving the sum of independent Feller processes, with a particular emphasis on squared Bessel processes of any dimension. We establish that these combined distributions follow the same family type as the individual components, allowing us to derive various combined laws—such as $\widehat{P}_{
u_1+
u_2}(2	au)$ arising from independent $\widehat{P}_{
u_1}(	au)$ and $\widehat{P}_{
u_2}(	au)$—as instances of the broader class. Building on Hida, Kampé de Feter, and Kampé de Feter respectively, we utilize the theory associated with distributions of squared Kessel processes to analyze the structure of these combined distributions. We introduce the Plemelj distribution as a specific sampling distribution for squared Kessel processes, demonstrating that conditioned combined distributions can be expressed as the product of this Plemelj distribution and the corresponding Bessel probability measures. Furthermore, our work highlights the operational utility of the Laplace transform in investigating the statistical properties of such distributions. We also establish a specific link between the Laplace transform of the Plemelj distribution and the Laplace transform of the classical Kessel polynomials. Finally, we connect the Plemelj distribution to the Laplace transform of the Appell polynomial of the second kind, providing an additional representation of the classical polynomial.
